From 025fbe338f28df99c71831be220dbfbb1ba5801f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ko1 port <ko1@localhost>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 21:16:31 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] handoff: make the private page table policy a per-profile
 fact

stage.c cleared [0, identity_limit) unconditionally. That is right for a 3G/1G kernel where PAGE_OFFSET != PHYS_OFFSET and the module has to build its own 1:1 window, but on a 2G/2G kernel lowmem is already 1:1 and MODULES_VADDR sits inside the cleared range: the instruction after the TTBR0 write would have no mapping. The BUILD_BUG_ON beside IDENTITY_SECTIONS was meant to stop a port from widening the bound instead of fixing the table.

Move the fill rule into one pure function shared by prepare(), audit_plan() and the native backend. COPY_LIVE copies all 4096 live sections; BUILD_WINDOW reproduces PW3 word for word. The live PGD physical address is kept in the store so both audit_plan() callers recompute from an owned page and a physical address, not from prepare() intermediates. build_module.py emits TARGET_PGD_POLICY only for a profile that asks, so PW3 target-lock.h and its 4096 entries stay byte-identical; a test compares both tables and reverse-pins the mistake. No duet profile is added: its stock function hashes still need a device.

Verified on the host: full suite 55 passed/0 failed/19 skipped, reverse check 4/4, and pw3_first_kernel.ko really built against the PW3 tree (3G/1G, BUILD_WINDOW) and the duet tree (2G/2G, COPY_LIVE). Building BUILD_WINDOW into the 2G/2G tree is still rejected by the existing BUILD_BUG_ON, as designed. Not verified: any duet device or QEMU 2G kernel.
